window.docContents[76].resolve({"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products/Proof-of-Theorem-3___10___2/#Chapter3___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3___10___2":{"contents":"Proof. (i) By the density theorem, the maps A \\to \\operatorname{End} V and B \\to \\operatorname{End} W are surjective. Therefore, the map A \\otimes B \\to \\operatorname{End} V \\otimes \\operatorname{End} W = \\operatorname{End}(V \\otimes W) is surjective. Thus, V \\otimes W is irreducible.\n\n(ii) First we show the existence of V and W. Let A', B' be the images of A, B in \\operatorname{End} M. Then A', B' are finite dimensional algebras, and M is a representation of A' \\otimes B', so we may assume without loss of generality that A and B are finite dimensional.\n\nIn this case, we claim that\n\n\\operatorname{Rad}(A \\otimes B) = \\operatorname{Rad}(A) \\otimes B + A \\otimes \\operatorname{Rad}(B).\nIndeed, denote the latter by J. Then J is a nilpotent ideal in A \\otimes B, as \\operatorname{Rad}(A) and \\operatorname{Rad}(B) are nilpotent. On the other hand, (A \\otimes B)/J = (A/\\operatorname{Rad}(A)) \\otimes (B/\\operatorname{Rad}(B)), which is a product of two semisimple algebras, hence semisimple. This implies J \\supset \\operatorname{Rad}(A \\otimes B). Altogether, by Proposition 3.5.3, we see that J = \\operatorname{Rad}(A \\otimes B), proving the claim.\n\nThus, we see that\n\n(A \\otimes B)/\\operatorname{Rad}(A \\otimes B) = A/\\operatorname{Rad}(A) \\otimes B/\\operatorname{Rad}(B).\n\nNow, M is an irreducible representation of (A \\otimes B)/\\operatorname{Rad}(A \\otimes B), so it is clearly of the form M = V \\otimes W, where V is an irreducible representation of A/\\operatorname{Rad}(A) and W is an irreducible representation of B/\\operatorname{Rad}(B). Also, V, W are uniquely determined by M (as all of the algebras involved are direct sums of matrix algebras). \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.10. Representations of tensor products\u0009Proof of Theorem 3.10.2","header":"Proof of Theorem 3.10.2","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products/Proof-of-Theorem-3___10___2/#Chapter3___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3___10___2"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Classification-of-subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/#Chapter3___Proposition3___1___4___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.1. Subrepresentations in semisimple representations\u0009Classification of subrepresentations in semisimple representations\u0009Classification of subrepresentations in semisimple representations","header":"Formalization","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Classification-of-subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/#Chapter3___Proposition3___1___4___formalization"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/Tensor-product-multiplicities-for-S_3___-S_4___-and-A_5/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables--Tensor-product-multiplicities-for-S_3___-S_4___-and-A_5--Tensor-product-multiplicities-for-S_3___-S_4___-and-A_5--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"Each tensor product in the second five-entry family is isomorphic to its multiplicity sum.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Example4.9.1; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.9. Computing tensor product multiplicities using character tables\u0009Tensor product multiplicities for S_3, S_4, and A_5\u0009Tensor product multiplicities for S_3, S_4, and A_5\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/Tensor-product-multiplicities-for-S_3___-S_4___-and-A_5/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables--Tensor-product-multiplicities-for-S_3___-S_4___-and-A_5--Tensor-product-multiplicities-for-S_3___-S_4___-and-A_5--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Section-5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___15___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.15. The Frobenius character formula\u0009Section 5.15: The Frobenius character formula\u0009Section 5.15: The Frobenius character formula","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Section-5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___15___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Section-5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___23___heading-1":{"contents":"","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.23. Algebraic representations of $GL(V)$\u0009Section 5.23: Algebraic representations of GL(V)\u0009Section 5.23: Algebraic representations of GL(V)","header":"5.23. Algebraic representations of  GL(V)","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Section-5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___23___heading-1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/Galois-extension-for-representations-and-vanishing-of-characters/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers--Galois-extension-for-representations-and-vanishing-of-characters--Galois-extension-for-representations-and-vanishing-of-characters--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"For a simple representation of dimension greater than one whose character never vanishes, the product of the squared character norms away from the identity lies strictly between zero and one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Problem5.2.7/Derived01; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.2. Algebraic numbers and algebraic integers\u0009Galois extension for representations and vanishing of characters\u0009Galois extension for representations and vanishing of characters\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/Galois-extension-for-representations-and-vanishing-of-characters/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers--Galois-extension-for-representations-and-vanishing-of-characters--Galois-extension-for-representations-and-vanishing-of-characters--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___3___-Frobenius-divisibility/Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___3___1-_LPAR_Frobenius-divisibility_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___3___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.3. Frobenius divisibility\u0009Completion of proof of Theorem 5.3.1 (Frobenius divisibility)\u0009Completion of proof of Theorem 5.3.1 (Frobenius divisibility)","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___3___-Frobenius-divisibility/Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___3___1-_LPAR_Frobenius-divisibility_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___3___1___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Burnside___s-theorem___-groups-of-order-p___a-q___b-are-solvable/#Chapter5___Theorem5___4___3":{"contents":"Theorem 5.4.3 (Burnside). Any group G of order p^a q^b, where p and q are primes and a, b \\geq 0, is solvable.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.4. Burnside's theorem\u0009Burnside's theorem: groups of order p^a q^b are solvable","header":"Burnside's theorem: groups of order p^a q^b are solvable","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Burnside___s-theorem___-groups-of-order-p___a-q___b-are-solvable/#Chapter5___Theorem5___4___3"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Group-with-conjugacy-class-of-size-p___k-is-not-simple/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Group-with-conjugacy-class-of-size-p___k-is-not-simple--Group-with-conjugacy-class-of-size-p___k-is-not-simple--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"A finite group with a conjugacy class of positive prime-power size has a nontrivial proper\nnormal subgroup.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5.4.3; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5.4.6; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.4.6; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.4. Burnside's theorem\u0009Group with conjugacy class of size p^k is not simple\u0009Group with conjugacy class of size p^k is not simple\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Group-with-conjugacy-class-of-size-p___k-is-not-simple/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Group-with-conjugacy-class-of-size-p___k-is-not-simple--Group-with-conjugacy-class-of-size-p___k-is-not-simple--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___9___-The-Frobenius-formula-for-the-character-of-an-induced-representation/Proof-of-Theorem-5___9___1-_LPAR_Frobenius-formula_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___9___1":{"contents":"Proof. For a right H-coset \\sigma of G, let us define\n\nV_\\sigma = \\{f \\in \\operatorname{Ind}_H^G V \\mid f(g) = 0 \\ \\forall g \\notin \\sigma\\}.\n\nThen one has\n\n\\operatorname{Ind}_H^G V = \\bigoplus_\\sigma V_\\sigma,\n\nand so\n\n\\chi(g) = \\sum_\\sigma \\chi_\\sigma(g),\n\nwhere \\chi_\\sigma(g) is the trace of the diagonal block of \\rho(g) corresponding to V_\\sigma.\n\nSince g(\\sigma) = \\sigma g is a right H-coset for any right H-coset \\sigma, \\chi_\\sigma(g) = 0 if \\sigma \\neq \\sigma g.\n\nNow assume that \\sigma = \\sigma g. Then x_\\sigma g = h x_\\sigma where h = x_\\sigma g x_\\sigma^{-1} \\in H. Consider the map \\alpha : V_\\sigma \\to V defined by \\alpha(f) = f(x_\\sigma). Since f \\in V_\\sigma is uniquely determined by f(x_\\sigma), \\alpha is an isomorphism. We have\n\n\\alpha(gf) = g(f)(x_\\sigma) = f(x_\\sigma g) = f(h x_\\sigma) = \\rho_V(h) f(x_\\sigma) = h\\alpha(f),\n\nand gf = \\alpha^{-1} h \\alpha(f). This means that \\chi_\\sigma(g) = \\chi_V(h). Therefore\n\n\\chi(g) = \\sum_{\\sigma \\in H \\backslash G, \\sigma g = \\sigma} \\chi_V(x_\\sigma g x_\\sigma^{-1}).\n\\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.9. The Frobenius formula for the character of an induced representation\u0009Proof of Theorem 5.9.1 (Frobenius formula)","header":"Proof of Theorem 5.9.1 (Frobenius formula)","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___9___-The-Frobenius-formula-for-the-character-of-an-induced-representation/Proof-of-Theorem-5___9___1-_LPAR_Frobenius-formula_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___9___1"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Example-6___2___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A1/#Chapter6___Example6___2___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.2. Indecomposable representations of the quivers $A_1$, $A_2$, $A_3$\u0009Example 6.2.2: Indecomposable representations of A1\u0009Example 6.2.2: Indecomposable representations of A1","header":"Formalization","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Example-6___2___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A1/#Chapter6___Example6___2___2___formalization"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/Section-7___5___-Representable-functors/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___5":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.5. Representable functors\u0009Section 7.5: Representable functors","header":"Section 7.5: Representable functors","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/Section-7___5___-Representable-functors/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___5"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/Classification-of-indecomposable-projective-modules/#Chapter9___Theorem9___2___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.2. Projective covers\u0009Classification of indecomposable projective modules\u0009Classification of indecomposable projective modules","header":"Formalization","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/Classification-of-indecomposable-projective-modules/#Chapter9___Theorem9___2___1___formalization"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Blocks-of-semisimple___-local___-and-specific-algebras/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___5___-Blocks--Blocks-of-semisimple___-local___-and-specific-algebras--Blocks-of-semisimple___-local___-and-specific-algebras--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"Establishes the displayed auxiliary relation between the two specified module-category objects.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Example9.5.2; role=primary]\n\nCharacterizes the displayed auxiliary relation between simple module-category objects over a semisimple ring by the existence of an isomorphism.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Example9.5.2; role=primary]\n\nEstablishes the displayed auxiliary relation between module-category objects induced by two simple modules.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Example9.5.2; role=primary]\n\nProvides the displayed auxiliary relation between two simple module-category objects over a local Artinian commutative ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Example9.5.2; role=primary]\n\nProduces an isomorphism between simple module-category objects over a semisimple ring from the displayed auxiliary relation.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Example9.5.2; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.5. Blocks\u0009Blocks of semisimple, local, and specific algebras\u0009Blocks of semisimple, local, and specific algebras\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Blocks-of-semisimple___-local___-and-specific-algebras/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___5___-Blocks--Blocks-of-semisimple___-local___-and-specific-algebras--Blocks-of-semisimple___-local___-and-specific-algebras--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/Backmatter/Mathematical-references/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--Backmatter--Mathematical-references":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u0009Backmatter","header":"Mathematical references","id":"/Backmatter/Mathematical-references/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--Backmatter--Mathematical-references"}});